          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01LFUh5YboQ7vpQjHYmnXAeq",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One authoritative source on hybrid retrieval patterns; core components are industry-standard.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets\\u2014often via reciprocal rank fusion\\u2014to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Why Hybrid?\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent. Sparse methods like BM25 excel at exact term matching and rare identifiers, while dense vector search captures semantic intent and paraphrases. Together, they cover both capabilities.\\n\\n### Production Adoption\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, replacing single-method approaches that risk either missing semantic matches (sparse-only) or retrieving irrelevant documents with surface similarity (dense-only).\\n\\n### Merging Strategies\\n\\n**Reciprocal Rank Fusion** is a common approach for merging ranked result sets from multiple retrievers. It avoids score normalization issues by using ranks instead of raw scores, making it robust across different scoring schemes.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval overview from production RAG system patterns.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
